// @trap
// THE CONSTRUCTION-TIME EXTENT CHECK. `f32view(off, count)` traps unless the
// whole range `[off, off + count*4)` lies inside the `Buf`. This is the ONE
// check that is taken per VIEW rather than per ACCESS, and it is what lets the
// per-access check be just `0 <= i < length`: if the extent was validated when
// the view was made, an in-range index cannot leave the `Buf`.
//
// It also puts the diagnosis at the right place. Without it, a view built one
// element too long over a 64-byte `Buf` is a perfectly ordinary value whose LAST
// element silently reads the next allocation — the failure would surface at an
// access, arbitrarily later, with nothing to say about the mistake. With it, the
// trap is at the line that got the arithmetic wrong.
//
// This is the same layering as `Buffer(n)` itself, which validates at ALLOCATION
// time and then never again (§J3: policy is std's, and the cheap place to spend
// it is the one that runs once).
//
// Inverted control: `buffer-view-extent-control.vl` pins the boundary case that
// must NOT trap — a view exactly filling the `Buf`, and an empty one.
import { Buffer, f32view } from "std:buffer"

const b = Buffer(64)
const x = b.f32view(0, 17) // 17 * 4 == 68 > 64
print(x.length)
